      "quote": "Nourish the roots, let the canopy fall\nWe don't need the power from the capital hall\nWhen the screens go dark and the water won't run\nI'll be down in the dirt, looking up at the sun",
      "story": "Track 15, \"Nourish the Roots,\" serves as the album's final thesis, but strips away any grandiosity. Instead of a soaring anthem, it is a gritty, foot-stomping, E-minor acoustic blues track. It frames the act of growing food and unplugging from the state's failing architecture as a quiet, fierce, and solitary rebellion. Combining the protagonist's past as an IT grid engineer with his present as a permaculture farmer, the song concludes that the only way to survive the crash of the empire's \"mainframe\" is to air-gap your life and put your hands in the dirt.\n\nHeavy porch-stomping kick drum, dirty acoustic slide guitar, percussive acoustic slapping\n\n16:9 high-contrast close-up. A heavy, mud-caked leather work boot stomps down firmly on dark earth. Beside it, a thick, severed black ethernet cable lies uselessly. Emerging from the soil inches away is a vibrant green sprout.\n\n[Intro]\n[Verse 1]\nThey\u2019re running the servers till the motherboards melt\nChasing a profit that they never even felt\nThe grid is overloading from the weight of the lies\nA multi-billion dollar system falling out of the skies\nI unplugged the terminal, I severed the wire\nI'm walking away from the digital fire\n[Chorus]\nNourish the roots, let the canopy fall\nWe don't need the power from the capital hall\nWhen the screens go dark and the water won't run\nI'll be down in the dirt, looking up at the sun\nJust a shovel and the mud and a heavy pair of boots\nLet the mainframe crash.\nWe're gonna nourish the roots.\n[Verse 2]\nThe neighbor on the left got the cameras on the gate\nThe neighbor on the right is just bending to the weight\nI'm standing in the middle with the water and the seed\nAir-gapping the garden from the engine of their greed\nYou can't patch a system that is engineered to break\nYou just gather up the pieces that the devil didn't take\n[Chorus]\nNourish the roots, let the canopy fall\nWe don't need the power from the capital hall\nWhen the screens go dark and the water won't run\nI'll be down in the dirt, looking up at the sun\nJust a shovel and the mud and a heavy pair of boots\nLet the mainframe crash.\nWe're gonna nourish the roots.\n[Bridge]\nYou can't reboot a dead planet.\nYou can't download a drop of rain.\nIf you want to survive the blackout...\nYou gotta step off the train!\n\n[Outro]\nAir-gap the soul.\nTurn the soil.\nNourish the roots.\n\u00a9 2026 The Shady River Bard. All rights reserved.",
